Mass-transfer effects on current distributions
↳ Mass-transfer-limited current distribution: a tertiary current distribution with \nabla c \neq 0, t_i \to 0 (neglect \nabla\phi) — also called “convective transport” problems.
Previously, i_{lim} \Rightarrow c_{lim} \Rightarrow 0 at the electrode, with a constant BL thickness \delta → results in a uniform current distribution.



                  Note



Flux is not the same as concentration! conc = 0 at the electrode [but there will be flux].


But the geometry and flow conditions of the cell may not always result in a constant \delta ↳ we need to understand how M-T impacts this and the current distribution.
Example: microfluidic electrochemical cell with 2 line electrodes
Sketch: a channel of height 2h (from y = 0 to y = 2h) with fluid flowing in x; the WE on the bottom wall and the CE on the top wall, both from x = 0 to x = L. In the z-direction the electrodes take up the full width, and w \gg h.

Assume laminar flow; we only have an x-velocity:

v_x(y) = \frac{3}{2}U\left(1 - \left(\frac{h - y}{h}\right)^2\right)\;;\qquad U = \frac{Q}{2hw}
↳ Governing equation for t_j = 0: convection–diffusion (migration neglected):
v_x\left(\frac{\partial c_j}{\partial x}\right) = D\left(\frac{\partial^2c_j}{\partial x^2} + \frac{\partial^2c_j}{\partial y^2}\right)
For fast kinetics, c_{lim} = 0 at all y = 0 on the electrode.
Flow in the x-direction is much greater than diffusion in the x-direction (convection dominates in the flow direction, according to Pe). In a microfluidic device (small Re) ↓ large Sc:
Pe = \frac{\text{convective transport}}{\text{diffusive transport}} = Sc\cdot Re\;;\qquad Sc = \frac{\nu}{D_{lim}} \sim 10^3 \text{ for small molecules in water}
Pe → large [convection dominates].
\therefore\quad v_x\frac{\partial c_{lim}}{\partial x} = D_{lim}\frac{\partial^2c_{lim}}{\partial y^2}\;;\qquad\text{BCs: } c_{lim} = c_\infty \text{ at } x = 0 \text{ (no reaction yet)};\;\; c_{lim} = c_\infty \text{ as } y \to \infty
The concentration should only vary within \delta near the electrode, so we can approximate the velocity there with a Taylor expansion:
v_x = \cancel{v_x|_{y=0}}^{\,\text{(no slip)}} + \left.\frac{\partial v_x}{\partial y}\right|_{y=0}y
Plugging in the laminar-flow equation: \dfrac{\partial v}{\partial y} = \dfrac{3U}{2}\left(-2\frac{(h - y)}{h}\left(-\frac{1}{h}\right)\right); at y = 0, \dfrac{\partial v_x}{\partial y} = \dfrac{3U}{h} \Rightarrow v_x = \dfrac{3Uy}{h}:
\boxed{\frac{3U}{h}y\frac{\partial c}{\partial x} = D\frac{\partial^2c}{\partial y^2}}
This is a PDE that can be solved with a “combination of variables” (similarity solution!). Let
\xi = y\left(\frac{U}{3hDx}\right)^{1/3}
→ this will make the equation have just one independent variable ↳ making it an ODE.
\frac{\partial c}{\partial x} = \frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi}\frac{\partial\xi}{\partial x}\;;\quad \frac{\partial\xi}{\partial x} = y\left(\frac{U}{3hD}\right)^{1/3}\left(-\frac{1}{3}\right)x^{-4/3} = -\frac{\xi}{3x} \;\Rightarrow\; \frac{\partial c}{\partial x} = \frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi}\left(\frac{-\xi}{3x}\right)
\frac{\partial c}{\partial y} = \frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi}\frac{\partial\xi}{\partial y}\;;\quad \frac{\partial\xi}{\partial y} = \left(\frac{U}{3hDx}\right)^{1/3} = \frac{\xi}{y}
\dfrac{\partial^2c}{\partial y^2} ← both the product and the chain rule:
\frac{\partial^2c}{\partial y^2} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi}\frac{\partial\xi}{\partial y}\right) = \frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi}\underbrace{\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{\partial\xi}{\partial y}\right)}_{0} + \frac{\partial\xi}{\partial y}\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi}\right) = \frac{\partial^2c}{\partial\xi^2}\left(\frac{\partial\xi}{\partial y}\right)^2 = \frac{\partial^2c}{\partial\xi^2}\left(\frac{U}{3hDx}\right)^{2/3}
Plugging into the original PDE and dividing by \left(\frac{U}{3hDx}\right)^{2/3}:
\frac{3U}{h}y\frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi}\left(\frac{-\xi}{3x}\right) = D\frac{\partial^2c}{\partial\xi^2}\left(\frac{U}{3hDx}\right)^{2/3}
\;\Rightarrow\;
3\underbrace{y\left(\frac{U}{3hDx}\right)^{1/3}}_{\xi}\frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi}(-\xi) = \frac{\partial^2c}{\partial\xi^2}
-3\xi^2\frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi} = \frac{\partial^2c}{\partial\xi^2} \;\Rightarrow\; \boxed{\frac{d^2c}{d\xi^2} + 3\xi^2\frac{dc}{d\xi} = 0}
BCs: at x = 0, c_{lim} = c_\infty \Rightarrow \xi \to \infty, c_{lim} = c_\infty; at y = \infty, c_{lim} = c_\infty \Rightarrow \xi \to \infty, c_{lim} = c_\infty; at y = 0, c_{lim} = 0 \Rightarrow \xi = 0, c_{lim} = 0.
Solving (the notes say using the Frobenius method):
\boxed{\frac{c_{lim}}{c_\infty} = \frac{1}{\Gamma\!\left(\frac{4}{3}\right)}\int_0^\xi e^{-x^3}\,dx}
→ Now we know the concentration profile in the BL. Solving for the flux (laminar flow, no convection in y):
N_y = -D\frac{\partial c}{\partial y} \;\Rightarrow\; D\left.\frac{\partial c}{\partial y}\right|_{y=0} = D\left.\frac{\partial c}{\partial\xi}\right|_{\xi=0}\left.\frac{\partial\xi}{\partial y}\right|_{y=0}
N_y = \frac{Dc_\infty}{\Gamma\!\left(\frac{4}{3}\right)}\underbrace{\frac{\partial}{\partial\xi}\left[\int_0^\xi e^{-x^3}dx\right]_{\xi=0}}_{\text{fundamental theorem of calculus}}\left(\frac{U}{3hDx}\right)^{1/3} = \frac{Dc_\infty}{\Gamma\!\left(\frac{4}{3}\right)}\left.e^{-\xi^3}\right|_{\xi=0}\left(\frac{U}{3hDx}\right)^{1/3}
(using \frac{\partial}{\partial\xi}\int_0^\xi f(x)\,dx = f(\xi).)
\boxed{N_y = -\frac{c_\infty D}{\Gamma\!\left(\frac{4}{3}\right)}\left(\frac{U}{3hDx}\right)^{1/3}}
↙ Find the current: \dfrac{s\,i}{nF} = -N_y. Plot \dfrac{i(x)}{i_{avg}}, with i_{avg} = \dfrac{1}{L}\displaystyle\int_0^L i\,dx.

