Two handwritten pages of mass-transfer notes, numbered 4 and 5 in the original.
Solid mass diffusivity
D_{AB} = \underbrace{D_0}_{\text{proportionality constant}}e^{-Q/RT}
Convective mass transfer
Mass transfer of a solute into a moving fluid:
\boxed{N_A = \underbrace{k_c}_{\text{convective mass transfer coeff.}}(C_{A,s} - C_{A,\infty})}
When any moving fluid encounters a solid ⇓ boundary layer (both velocity and conc. BL).
(sketch: flow v_\infty over a flat plate coated with solute A; the boundary layer grows along the plate, the flux N_A leaves the surface, where C_{A,s} = C_A^*.)
\therefore\;-N_A\cdot S = \frac{dm_A}{dt}\quad(\text{mass of A decreases until all is 0})\;\Rightarrow\;N_A = \frac{m_{A0}}{t\cdot S}\;\Rightarrow\;\boxed{k_c(C_{A,s} - C_{A,\infty}) = \frac{m_{A0}}{t\cdot S}}
\underbrace{Sc}_{\text{Schmidt number}} = \frac{\text{momentum diffusivity}}{\text{mass diffusivity}} = \frac{\overbrace{\mu/\rho}^{\text{kinematic viscosity}}}{D_{AB}}
(momentum diffusivity = diffusion due to pressure, shear stress) ↳ physically relates the thickness of the hydrodynamic layer &amp; the mass-transfer boundary layer.
Sh = \frac{\text{molecular MT resistance}}{\text{convective MT resistance}} = \frac{k_cL}{D_{AB}}
Laminar flow in the BL
Using a mass balance and BCs, we arrive at (and the Blasius approx.), for Sc = 1:
\frac{dC_A}{dy}\bigg|_{y=0} = (C_{A,\infty} - C_{A,s})\left(\frac{0.332\,Re_x^{1/2}}{x}\right)
As N_{A,y} = -D_{AB}\dfrac{\partial C_A}{\partial y}\Big|_{y=0} = k_c(C_{A,s} - C_{A,\infty}), we get (k_c is a function of x):
k_{c,x} = \frac{D_{AB}}{x}\left[0.332\,Re_x^{1/2}\right]\quad\text{or}\quad\boxed{\frac{k_{c,x}x}{D_{AB}} = Sh_x = 0.332\,Re_x^{1/2}}
When Sc \neq 1: \dfrac{\delta}{\delta_c} = Sc^{1/3} (\delta → hydrodynamic BL; \delta_c → conc. BL) &amp; Re_x &lt; 2.0\times10^5 [laminar]:
\frac{\partial C_A}{\partial y}\bigg|_{y=0} = (C_{A,\infty} - C_{A,s})\left(\frac{0.332\,Re_x^{1/2}Sc^{1/3}}{x}\right)\;\therefore\;\boxed{\frac{k_{c,x}x}{D_{AB}} = Sh_x = 0.332\,Re_x^{1/2}Sc^{1/3}}
We can also determine the average mass-transfer coefficient:
k_c = \frac{\int_0^Lk_{c,x}(x)\,dx}{\int_0^Ldx} = 0.664\frac{D_{AB}}{L}\underbrace{\left(\frac{v_\infty L}{\nu}\right)^{1/2}}_{Re}Sc^{1/3} = 2\,Sh_x|_{x=L}
Transcriber’s note: the last equality is as written; 0.664\,\frac{D_{AB}}{L}Re_L^{1/2}Sc^{1/3} equals 2k_{c,x}|_{x=L} (twice the local coefficient at x = L), i.e. Sh_L = 2Sh_x|_{x=L} when made dimensionless.
However, when the flow is not laminar or the surface is not a flat plate, then we use the von Kármán approximation. (sketch: a control volume inside a growing boundary layer with flows in and out.)
